      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="video-cap">The Garage run used for every frame on this page.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l15-hero-garage.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 15 Garage: a blue saloon on yellow-marked concrete in front of shelving units, a workbench and stacked crates, with a red saloon behind and a roll-up door on the left" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>A working garage rather than a storage closet. The concrete is painted with yellow parking and service lines, a striped roll-up door takes up the left wall, and the back wall is a long run of metal shelving loaded with boxes, cans, blue bins and tools. Two desks with monitors sit against the shelving, which is what makes the room feel like an office bolted onto a workshop. A traffic cone, red gas cans, a rolling blue cart and a tyre stack fill the gaps.</p>
    <p>The three cars are the level's organising idea. A white saloon parks on the left, a red saloon sits on a lift in the middle, and a blue saloon straddles a service pit on the right, with tyres stacked down in the pit and piles of dirt or sand at its edges. Every car close-up in the recording is framed on one of these three, and the framed screen on the back desk repeats the same three colours — which is as close as the room comes to writing an instruction down.</p>
    <p>Structurally it is a hunt, not a chain. Unlike the Storage Room, the props here are spread over a wide floor plan, and the recording moves between the cart, the desk screen, the shape board and the lock without any visible dependency between them. That makes it a room you can clear in any order — but it also makes it the room where it is easiest to finish the visible puzzles and still be missing a pickup.</p>

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Everything we could identify, and what it is for.</p>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Where</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>White saloon</td><td>Left bay, on yellow lines</td><td>Parked, not lifted. One of the three poster colours.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Red saloon</td><td>Centre bay, on the lift</td><td>Raised on the lift; its nose is a close-up at 4:19.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Blue saloon</td><td>Right bay, over the service pit</td><td>Tyres stacked in the pit below, dirt piles at the rim.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Framed screen</td><td>Back desk, above a lamp and a shelf of books</td><td>Three sports cars: yellow with raised doors, red, blue.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Utility cart</td><td>Foreground, blue handles</td><td>Green <b>TECHNOLOGY</b> hardbacks and flat blue rings.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shape board</td><td>Full-screen close-up</td><td>Five framed panels; only the leftmost square cycles colour.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Four-letter lock</td><td>Yellow panel, dark tiles in gold frames</td><td>Reads <b>L S Z A</b> left to right.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Batteries</td><td>Inventory pickup</td><td>Two red batteries, counted as <b>2/2</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Piece of paper</td><td>Inventory item</td><td>Ladder-like diagram on a brown ground; labelled <b>Piece of paper</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Service pit</td><td>Under the blue saloon</td><td>Opening in the concrete with stacked tyres inside.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Roll-up door</td><td>Left wall</td><td>Yellow and black hazard stripes; opens late in the run.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shelving run</td><td>Back wall, full width</td><td>Boxes, cans, blue bins, tools — the room's visual noise.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The order below follows the recording. Timestamps are video time; the links jump to the matching second.</p>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:00</span>
      <strong>Title card, then straight into the bays</strong>
      The level opens on <b>Level 15 / Garage</b> over the full room, then the card lifts and you are looking at the three bays. Read the floor before you read the shelves: the yellow lines tell you which car is parked, which is lifted and which is over the pit, and that ordering matters later when the poster asks you to match colours.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l15-level-title.jpg" alt="The Level 15 Garage title card in Rooms and Exits Chapter 3, laid over the three-bay garage" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJUnv1xKrjE&t=0s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:00</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:29 · the cart</span>
      <strong>Green hardbacks labelled TECHNOLOGY</strong>
      A blue-handled metal cart sits in the foreground with several green hardback books inside, each carrying the word <b>TECHNOLOGY</b> on the cover, plus a stack of flat blue rings. Behind the cart: a metal crate of cardboard boxes, a length of blue fabric draped over it, and a pair of work boots on their sides on the concrete. A yellow line curves across the floor under the wheels. Books with a printed word on them are rare in this game, so that word is the one piece of text in the room that is not a number.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l15-cart-technologies.jpg" alt="A blue-handled utility cart in the Garage holding green hardback books labelled TECHNOLOGY and a stack of flat blue rings" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJUnv1xKrjE&t=29s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:29</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 1:59 · the poster</span>
      <strong>Three sports cars, in the room's three colours</strong>
      Over the desk lamp and a shelf of books hangs a framed screen showing three sports cars in a row: a <b>yellow</b> car with its doors raised like wings, a <b>red</b> car in the middle, and a <b>blue</b> car on the right. Compare that to the floor — white, red, blue. Red and blue line up exactly; yellow does not appear on any car in the room. That mismatch is the sort of thing the game plants on purpose, so note it rather than assuming the poster is a straight key.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l15-car-screen.jpg" alt="The framed screen in the Garage showing three sports cars: a yellow car with raised doors, a red car and a blue car" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJUnv1xKrjE&t=119s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:59</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 2:39 · the paper</span>
      <strong>A ladder-shaped diagram in the inventory</strong>
      The <b>Piece of paper</b> pickup is a brown, textured sheet with a line drawing on it: a run of horizontal rungs between two verticals, reading like a ladder or a stylised letter E. The inventory labels it plainly — <b>Piece of paper</b> — and offers the usual Use, Combine, Dismantle and Examine actions. Whatever it maps onto is not shown in the same frame, so treat it as a shape to carry, not a code to read.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l15-piece-of-paper.jpg" alt="The Piece of paper inventory item in the Garage showing a ladder-shaped diagram on a brown sheet" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJUnv1xKrjE&t=159s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:39</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 2:59 · the shape board, first state</span>
      <strong>A purple square with a dark circle</strong>
      Five framed panels in a row. On the left a <b>purple</b> square holding a dark grey circle; next to it two solid <b>green</b> squares; on the right a stack of two horizontal bars, the top <b>red</b> and the bottom <b>green</b>. This is a state puzzle in the purest form — no numbers, no letters, just a small set of attributes that can be changed. The four panels on the right never move in the recording.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l15-shape-grid.jpg" alt="The Garage shape board in its first state: a purple square with a dark circle, two green squares, a red bar and a green bar" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJUnv1xKrjE&t=179s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:59</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 3:19 · the shape board, second state</span>
      <strong>The left panel cycles to blue</strong>
      Twenty seconds later the leftmost square has gone from purple with a dark circle to <b>blue</b> with a dark circle. The two green squares and the red and green bars are untouched, which tells you the board is not a shared grid — only the left panel is being driven. By the next sampled second it has moved again, and the circle's fill appears to flip along with the background colour — treat that later value as a trend rather than a confirmed reading.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l15-shape-grid-set.jpg" alt="The Garage shape board in its second state, with the leftmost square now blue and the two green squares and coloured bars unchanged" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJUnv1xKrjE&t=199s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:19</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 3:59 · the batteries</span>
      <strong>A set of two, counted 2/2</strong>
      The inventory at this point holds exactly one entry: a stack of <b>two red batteries</b>, tagged <b>2/2</b>. The counter matters — this game uses a fraction when an item is a matched set, and a set of two usually means a device that will not accept one. Everything else the player has picked up by now has already been spent, which is why the grid is otherwise empty.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l15-battery-inventory.jpg" alt="The Garage inventory showing a single stack of two red batteries counted as 2/2" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJUnv1xKrjE&t=239s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:59</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 4:19 · the red car</span>
      <strong>The nose of the lifted saloon</strong>
      The camera drops onto the front of the red saloon on the lift — bonnet, headlights, part of a wheel, all framed by corner brackets. It is a plain inspection view rather than a puzzle surface: there is no badge to read or dial to turn in the frame. Its value is positional. Together with the white car on the left and the blue car over the pit, it completes the three-car set the poster was showing you.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l15-red-car-front.jpg" alt="A close-up of the front of the red saloon on the lift in the Garage, showing the bonnet, headlights and a wheel" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJUnv1xKrjE&t=259s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:19</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 5:09 · the room at full stretch</span>
      <strong>Everything in one frame</strong>
      Late in the run the camera pulls back to the widest view in the level: the blue saloon centred on its pit, the red car behind it, shelving running the full back wall, the workbench and monitor to the left, blue bins and crates stacked along the right. Use a frame like this to check what you have not touched yet — the room is wide enough that a missed pickup hides easily behind a crate.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l15-hero-garage.jpg" alt="A wide view of the Garage with the blue saloon on its service pit, the red saloon behind, shelving along the back wall and a workbench to the left" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJUnv1xKrjE&t=309s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:09</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 5:19 · the letter lock</span>
      <strong>L S Z A</strong>
      A yellow panel carrying four dark tiles in thin gold frames, one letter each. Read left to right, twice, from the whole frame and from a crop: <b>L</b>, <b>S</b>, <b>Z</b>, <b>A</b>. No glyph on the panel is ambiguous — the Z is the only one that could be mistaken for a 2, and it has the flat top and bottom strokes of a letter, not a numeral. This is the only code-shaped surface in the Garage.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l15-letter-lock.jpg" alt="The Garage four-letter lock, a yellow panel with four dark tiles reading L S Z A" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJUnv1xKrjE&t=319s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:19</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 5:31 · the last second</span>
      <strong>The banner lands, the numbers do not</strong>
      The screen cuts to <b>LEVEL COMPLETED!</b> with gold coins showering down and a reward card in the lower right that carries an eye symbol inside a blue frame. Then the file ends at 5:32, one second later, before the reward panel has drawn its figures. So the room is genuinely finished on camera — you can see the banner — but the coin total and the time never appear.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l15-level-complete.jpg" alt="The Garage completion banner in Rooms and Exits, showing Level Completed with falling coins and a reward card, before the reward figures resolve" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJUnv1xKrjE&t=331s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:31</a></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>You read the poster as a straight key and it does not work.</b> The screen shows yellow, red, blue; the floor has white, red, blue. Two of the three match, one does not. If a puzzle in the Garage refuses the yellow-red-blue order, that is why — the poster is a mirror with one colour swapped, not a copy.</p>
    <p><b>You keep pressing the whole shape board.</b> Only the leftmost square changes across the sampled states. The two green squares and the red and green bars hold their values for the entire run, so time spent cycling the right-hand panels is time wasted.</p>
    <p><b>You take one battery.</b> The counter reads <b>2/2</b>, which means the game hands you a matched pair. If a device in this room will not accept the batteries, the usual cause is that the counter has not reached full — check the fraction, not the icon.</p>
    <p><b>You look for a keypad under the bonnet.</b> The car close-ups are inspection views. There is no dial, no plate and no number on the red saloon's nose; the car's only job is to be one of three colours.</p>
    <p><b>You wait for the reward screen.</b> It never arrives. The clip ends one second after the banner. If you are comparing coin payouts across Chapter 3, the Garage is not a usable data point from this recording.</p>

    <h2>Where the Garage sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and it lists twenty-four rooms. Garage is room <b>15 of 24</b>, straight after the Storage Room and immediately before the Attic. It is the last of the chapter's three utility spaces — cellar, garage, attic — and the widest of the three, which is why it is also the one where a missed pickup is easiest to hide.</p>
    <p>Mechanically it drops the Storage Room's colour bars and gives you a shape board instead. Same family of logic, different surface: instead of reading six bar heights you are watching one panel cycle through background colours and circle fills. If the Storage Room's switchboard made sense to you, this room will too.</p>

