    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Four things, stated plainly so you know which parts of this page are reads and which are reconstruction.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. The reward and the time.</b> The banner is real — <b>LEVEL COMPLETED!</b> is on screen at 5:31 and coins are already falling — but the file ends at 5:32, before the reward panel draws its coin figure and time. This page therefore prints no payout for the Garage. Any number quoted elsewhere is not from this recording.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. Whether L S Z A is the solution or a mid-attempt.</b> The letters themselves are solid: two passes, whole frame and crop, agreed on all four. What the footage cannot tell us is whether the player had already solved the lock or was still cycling it when the clip cut. Treat <b>L S Z A</b> as the value to try.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. What the shape board is for.</b> We can describe the two states precisely — purple with a dark circle, then blue with a dark circle, then red with a white circle — and we can say that only the leftmost panel moves. We cannot say what target the board is being driven toward, because no frame in the recording shows it locking or unlocking anything.
      <br><br>
      <b>4. The batteries' destination.</b> The <b>2/2</b> count is clear and the batteries are the only item in the inventory at that moment. What is not in the recording is a frame of them being fitted into a device, so the sentence "they go in X" cannot be written honestly. The same applies to the ladder-shaped <b>Piece of paper</b>, whose matching surface never appears on camera.
    </div>
